            "text": "{\"primary_meaning\":\"The realm of spiritual light, truth, and righteousness, often contrasted with the darkness or 'night' of sin, ignorance, and evil.\",\"primary_basis\":[\"1 Thessalonians 5:5\",\"Romans 13:12-13\",\"John 9:4\"],\"alternatives\":[{\"meaning\":\"The 'Day of the Lord' as the eschatological culmination of history, denoting ultimate divine judgment, vindication, and salvation.\",\"basis\":[\"Amos 5:18-20\",\"Joel 2:1-2\",\"Zephaniah 1:14-15\",\"1 Corinthians 3:13\"],\"prominence\":\"dominant\"},{\"meaning\":\"An extended epoch or era of time, particularly in interpretations of the creation week (Day-Age theory) or historical dispensations.\",\"basis\":[\"Genesis 1\",\"Psalm 90:4\",\"2 Peter 3:8\"],\"prominence\":\"common\"},{\"meaning\":\"A prophetic year, as utilized in the year-day principle for calculating apocalyptic timelines in historicist interpretation.\",\"basis\":[\"Numbers 14:34\",\"Ezekiel 4:6\",\"Daniel 9:24\"],\"prominence\":\"minority\"}],\"has_settled_consensus\":false,\"confidence\":\"high\",\"notes\":\"The symbolic meaning of 'day' is heavily context-dependent in Scripture. In moral and ethical teachings, it represents holiness and spiritual awakening. In prophetic and eschatological contexts, it refers to God's final judgment and intervention. In apocalyptic prophecy, it is sometimes used as a formulaic placeholder for a literal year.\"}",
